{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And he shall lay his hand upon the head of the burnt offering.</b> 'His hand' — one hand is meant. But concerning the scapegoat it is written, ״וְסָמַךְ אַהֲרֹן אֶת שְׁתֵּי יָדָיו״ — 'Aaron shall lean both his hands,' and the Ramban explained that the leaning for the burnt offering was also with both hands. And what can I do, when 'his hand' — one hand is meant? And that which seems right to me to say on this is: since every sin of thought is without deed, but every sin of deed is not without a preceding thought — for without this he does not intend the transgression at all, and it is coercion, not inadvertence. Therefore for the burnt offering, which comes for thought alone, it suffices to lean one hand, to place the trace and taint of the sin of his thought upon the animal. But the scapegoat, which bore upon it the iniquities of the children of Israel — both the sin of thought and the sin of deed — Aaron leaned both his hands upon it, to place upon it the trace and taint of these two kinds of iniquity." "And so we find in the portion of Pinchas (Numbers 27:18) concerning Joshua, that the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: ״קַח לְךָ אֶת יְהוֹשֻׁעַ בִּן נוּן אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר רוּחַ בּוֹ וְסָמַכְתָּ אֶת יָדְךָ עָלָיו״ — one hand is meant. But in actuality it is stated (Numbers 27:23): ״וַיִּסְמֹךְ אֶת יָדָיו עָלָיו״ — two hands are meant. And so here, in the commandment it is stated ״וְסָמַךְ יָדוֹ״, and in actuality it is written ״וְסָמַךְ אַהֲרֹן אֶת שְׁתֵּי יָדָיו״. And so in the portion of Pinchas, the passage of the constant burnt offering was juxtaposed to the ordination of Joshua — only to tell you that just as the constant offerings bear upon them the guilt of the people, for the morning constant offering atones for transgressments of the night and the afternoon constant offering atones for transgressments of the whole day, and this is the reason for semikhah: to place the trace and taint of all Israel upon the offering — so too the leaders of Israel are a guilt-offering for the people, as our Sages of blessed memory taught (Sifrei Deuteronomy 1:13) from the verse ״וַאֲשִׂימֵם בְּרָאשֵׁיכֶם״, that the guilt of the people is suspended upon their heads. And this is the reason for laying a hand upon Joshua: to say, the guilt of the people is suspended upon you — both the sin of deed and the sin of thought. And this applies specifically to 'a man in whom is spirit,' like Joshua, who knows how to walk against the spirit of each and every person, for it is in his power to perceive, through the holy spirit upon him, all that rises upon the spirit of each and every person. And when he sees that a wayward spirit is mingled within him — 'not in the spirit of HaShem' (I Kings 22:22) — then it is incumbent upon him to turn him back from his path and rebuke him and chastise him. And if he does not rebuke him, then the iniquity is suspended upon him. But a man in whom there is no spirit, who cannot go against the spirit of each and every person, is not held accountable for the sin of thought of his fellow, for 'the hidden things belong to HaShem our Elohim' (Deuteronomy 29:28), and we have no way of knowing how far the spirit and thought of one's fellow reaches." "Therefore it says: 'Take for yourself Joshua son of Nun, a man in whom is spirit' — who can walk, through the holy spirit upon him, against the spirit of each and every person, and knows even their hidden treasures. Therefore it is just that he should bear upon him the iniquity of the people, including that which arises from their spirit. And to this alludes what He said: 'And you shall lean your hand upon him' — for if not, what connection has 'in whom is spirit' with 'you shall lean your hand'? Rather, certainly the one depends upon the other. And the Holy One, blessed be He, did not need to command him to lean a second hand upon him as well, in order to place upon him also the trace and taint of the sin of deed of the people, for it is simple: if it is in his power to turn back many from the iniquity of the sin of deed and he does not do so — plainly he bears the iniquity of the many. And from this Moses learned that at the moment of ordination he leaned both his hands upon him, to place upon him the guilt of the people, whether they sin in thought or whether they sin in deed. And he is precisely the model of the offering that is leaned upon, which bears upon it the guilt of the people while all Israel remain clean — and this is a precious interpretation. And what it says, ״וְנִרְצָה לוֹ לְכַפֵּר עָלָיו״, in its plain sense means that this semikhah is the essence of atonement, through placing one's guilt upon the animal." "And by way of allusion we may say: ״וְסָמַךְ יָדוֹ עַל רֹאשׁ הָעֹלָה״ includes every 'olah' — both the animal, which is the 'olah upon its burning-place,' and the man who is leaned upon, who ascends to whatever rung, for the reason explained above. But given the greatness of the punishment laid upon the one leaned upon — who would be so foolish as to come forward to receive the semikhah, since it is not compensated for any loss? Therefore it says ״וְנִרְצָה לוֹ״ — he will presumably be willing to accept it in order to achieve atonement through it. As is concluded in Sanhedrin (14a): Rabbi Zeira would hide and conceal himself in order to be ordained, for he said: let me accept and fulfill, for he heard that which Rabbi Elazar said — a man does not rise to greatness unless all his iniquities are forgiven him — so he directed his soul toward it. And this is a precious derash." "<b>Before HaShem, and he shall lean.</b> Rashi explained: there is no semikhah on a bannah, for bannah means a high place. And our Sages of blessed memory said (Sotah 4b): whoever has haughtiness of spirit, it is as though he built a bannah, as it is stated (Isaiah 2:22): ״חִדְלוּ לָכֶם מִן הָאָדָם אֲשֶׁר נְשָׁמָה בְּאַפּוֹ כִּי בַמֶּה נֶחְשָׁב הוּא״ — do not read 'בַמֶּה' but 'בָּמָה.' For whoever builds a bannah for himself — his spirit becomes high and haughty upon him. For so we find in the language of our Sages of blessed memory (Chagigah 22a): 'that no person go and build a bannah for himself.' Therefore it is not 'before HaShem' from two sides: both from the side that the Shekhinah does not dwell except in a place where humility is found, as it is stated (Isaiah 57:15): ״אֶשְׁכּוֹן אֶת דַּכָּא״ — as was explained above at the end of the portion of Yitro (Exodus 20:19) and in the portion of Miketz (Genesis 49:1), that the great Name signifies the essence of humility; and the matter of the bannah will be explained, with God's help, further on in the portion of Matot, regarding the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Nedarim 22a): 'whoever takes a vow, it is as though he built a bannah'; and from the side that the Shekhinah does not dwell except in a place where all Israel are in one union, all gathering to one sanctuary — and not at a time when each person goes and builds a bannah for himself. As in the parable of the two ships, which they supported with the verse (Amos 9:6): ״הַבּוֹנֶה בַשָּׁמַיִם מַעֲלוֹתָיו וַאֲגֻדָּתוֹ עַל אֶרֶץ יְסָדָהּ״ (Yalkut Shimoni Amos 5145). And therefore the offering of the bannah does not remove the iniquity entirely, and therefore there is no semikhah in it."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :register {:flags {4 ["God"]}}, :notes {4 "I read במה throughout as 'bannah' (the technical term for a private altar) to preserve the Kli Yakar's wordplay linking bamah (high place) with height/haughtiness. The parable of two ships referenced via Yalkut Shimoni is a well-known rabbinic image: two ships sailing together appear stable, but a lone ship listing badly is steadied only by a heavy load — hence God 'founds His agudah (bond) on earth' so Israel's unity keeps the world steady."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:35:27.387665798Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}